---
// The one piece of arithmetic that gets a section to itself.
//
// It sits after the chain, not inside it: yield is a mechanism the whole loop
// leans on rather than a step you perform, and folding it in as step 4b made
// the chain stop moving. Here it reads as "and here is the part that is
// actually hard", which is what it is.
//
// EVERY CLAIM BELOW IS READ OFF THE CODE, not off a feature list. Verified on
// develop 2026-08-02:
//
//   core/engine.ts, ingredientLineQty()
//     `ap = ep / yieldFraction(line.yieldPct, recipeName)`, and its comment
//     says both expansion and costing call it "so yield/conversion behavior can
//     never diverge between them". That is the load-bearing claim of this whole
//     section: the yield moves the QUANTITY, not just the money. A tool that
//     gets the cost right and the quantity wrong still leaves you short at 5am.
//
//   server/db/schema.ts:114
//     `defaultYieldPct: real('default_yield_pct').notNull().default(100)`.
//     Nothing is assumed. Every yield is 100 percent until a human sets it,
//     which is why the copy says so rather than implying a stocked database.
//
//   components/ui/ReferenceChip.svelte
//     sourceLabel = 'USDA reference'. A suggestion that names its source and
//     applies only on tap.
//
//   core/learned-recipe-yield.ts
//     MIN_LEARNED_YIELD_EVENTS = 5, LEARNED_YIELD_DIRECTION_TOLERANCE = 0.05,
//     LEARNED_YIELD_DIRECTION_AGREEMENT = 0.8, materiality checked through
//     packsForNeed, and "Price is intentionally absent: a supplier move can
//     never create this signal." The 5-event floor carries the comment "one
//     unusual night cannot establish a recipe pattern".
//
//   components/recipes/LearnedYieldReview.svelte:108
//     The limitation sentence, quoted VERBATIM below. It is quoted rather than
//     paraphrased on purpose: a product that publishes the limits of its own
//     inference is making the strongest available argument that the rest of its
//     numbers are honest, and paraphrasing would cost that nothing but soften it.
//
// NOT SHOWN, and the section says so: the learned-yield review panel itself.
// It needs five or more clean events on one recipe and the walkthrough seed has
// three orders, so there is no honest capture of it. Recipes 1, 2, 3 and 5 were
// all checked on the running instance. A mocked screen here would undercut the
// exact claim the section is making.

/**
 * The three layers, shallowest first. Deliberately in this order: the reader
 * has to accept that trim is charged at all before "it also suggests a number"
 * means anything, and both of those before a paragraph about what the software
 * refuses to do on thin evidence can land as restraint rather than as excuses.
 */
const layers = [
	{
		tag: 'YOU SET IT',
		title: 'The yield lives on the line, and you can always overrule it',
		body: 'Every ingredient starts at 100 percent, which is to say the software assumes nothing about your kitchen until somebody tells it something. Set a yield on a recipe line and two things move together: what the plate costs, and how much you buy. They are computed by the same function, so they cannot drift apart.'
	},
	{
		tag: 'IT OFFERS',
		title: 'It suggests a number and names where the number came from',
		body: 'Where a reference figure exists it is offered beside the field, labelled as what it is, a USDA reference. You tap it or you ignore it. Nothing is ever filled in behind your back, because a yield you did not choose is a number you will not trust at the moment it matters.'
	},
	{
		tag: 'IT LEARNS',
		title: 'And it watches what you actually buy, quietly',
		body: 'After five or more clean events on a recipe, if four in five missed the same way and the difference is worth a whole pack, it asks. Your sheet says this batch needs eight pounds; you have been buying nine and a half; update the yield? It shows you the events it counted. It never changes anything on its own.'
	}
];

/**
 * What it refuses to do, which is the actual differentiator. Every other tool
 * says it learns. Almost none say what they will not conclude, and the
 * thresholds below are the interesting part of the design.
 */
const refusals = [
	{
		rule: 'Five events, minimum',
		why: 'One unusual night cannot establish a recipe pattern.'
	},
	{
		rule: 'Four in five agreeing',
		why: 'A split result is noise, and noise is not evidence of anything.'
	},
	{
		rule: 'Within five percent is nothing',
		why: 'A line that close to its sheet is neutral, not a direction.'
	},
	{
		rule: 'Worth a whole pack, or silence',
		why: 'If correcting it would not change what you buy, it is not worth your attention.'
	},
	{
		rule: 'Price is not part of the signal',
		why: 'A supplier putting their price up can never look like a yield problem.'
	}
];
---

<section id="yield" class="container-page border-t border-hairline py-section">
	<div class="max-w-2xl" data-reveal>
		<p class="eyebrow">The hard part</p>
		<h2 class="mt-4 text-h2 font-semibold">The case has the skins on.</h2>
		<p class="mt-5 text-lede text-ink-soft">
			Your recipe says five pounds of peeled shallots. The case you buy is five pounds
			eleven ounces with the skins on, and you paid for the skins.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-5 text-ink-soft">
			Most sheets handle this by adding ten percent for waste at the end. That is about
			right on average and wrong on every line. Shallots do not behave like a case of
			romaine, and the two errors do not cancel out. They move your real cost somewhere
			you cannot see it.
		</p>
	</div>

	<!-- The screenshot leads rather than illustrates: three columns in one row
	     make the argument faster than the paragraph above can, and a reader who
	     stops here has still got the point. -->
	<figure class="mt-10" data-reveal>
		<img
			src="/proof/yield-lines-mobile.png"
			alt="A recipe lines table on a phone. Roma tomato uses 110 grams, has a 91 percent yield, and 121 grams are bought. Red onion uses 25 grams at 88 percent yield and buys 28 grams. Cucumber sits at 90 percent. Feta, olive oil and oregano are at 100 percent, so what is used and what is bought match."
			width={780}
			height={1688}
			loading="lazy"
			decoding="async"
			class="h-auto w-full border border-hairline bg-paper shadow-ticket sm:hidden"
		/>
		{/* 982px is 1:1 for a 2x capture. The width/height attributes carry the FILE
		    size, not the CSS size. Declaring the CSS size there happened to keep the
		    aspect ratio right, so nothing shifted and the only symptom was the table
		    rendering at half scale with nothing obviously wrong.
		    (This comment lives outside the attribute list on purpose: Astro parses a
		    block comment placed among attributes AS attributes.) */}
		<img
			src="/proof/yield-lines.png"
			alt="A recipe lines table with columns for ingredient, used (EP), yield, to buy (AP), prep and cost. Roma tomato: 110 grams used, 91 percent yield, 121 grams to buy, cored and wedged, $0.33, 21 percent of plate cost. Cucumber: 0.4 each at 90 percent, peeled into half-moons, $0.49. Red onion: 25 grams used, 88 percent yield, 28 grams to buy, shaved thin, $0.06. Feta at 45 grams, olive oil at 12 millilitres and oregano at 1 gram are all 100 percent, so used and bought match. Base cost is $1.60."
			width={1964}
			height={988}
			loading="lazy"
			decoding="async"
			style="width: 982px; max-width: 100%"
			class="hidden h-auto border border-hairline bg-paper shadow-ticket sm:block"
		/>
		<figcaption class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-ink-soft">
			Used, yield, bought. Three columns in one row: the pot needs 110 grams of tomato,
			the tomato gives up 91 percent of itself, so 121 grams goes on the list. The
			difference is small on one plate and it is a case at two hundred.
		</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<ol role="list" class="mt-14" data-reveal>
		{
			layers.map((layer, i) => (
				<li class="border-t border-hairline-strong py-7 lg:py-8">
					<div class="grid gap-x-10 gap-y-3 lg:grid-cols-[3fr_5fr]">
						<div class="flex items-baseline gap-3">
							<span
								class="font-mono text-eyebrow font-semibold tabular-nums text-ink-soft"
								aria-hidden="true"
							>
								{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}
							</span>
							<div>
								<p class="font-mono text-eyebrow font-semibold tracking-wide text-amber-deep">
									{layer.tag}
								</p>
								<h3 class="mt-2 text-h3 font-semibold">{layer.title}</h3>
							</div>
						</div>
						<p class="max-w-xl text-ink-soft lg:pt-1">{layer.body}</p>
					</div>
				</li>
			))
		}
	</ol>

	<!-- The refusals get the softgreen panel, which is the page's device for "read
	     this bit". Marketing the restraint rather than the intelligence is the
	     whole play: every tool claims to learn, and the thresholds are what
	     separate a suggestion worth reading from a machine that nags. -->
	<div class="mt-14 bg-softgreen p-8 lg:p-10" data-reveal>
		<h3 class="text-h3 font-semibold">What it refuses to conclude</h3>
		<p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-ink-soft">
			Five conditions, all of which have to hold before it will say a word. They are the
			reason a suggestion from this thing is worth reading.
		</p>
		<dl class="mt-8 grid gap-x-16 gap-y-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
			{
				refusals.map((item) => (
					<div class="flex gap-3">
						<span
							class="mt-2.5 size-1.5 shrink-0 rotate-45 bg-amber-deep"
							aria-hidden="true"
						/>
						<div>
							<dt class="font-semibold">{item.rule}</dt>
							<dd class="mt-1 text-ink-soft">{item.why}</dd>
						</div>
					</div>
				))
			}
		</dl>
	</div>

	<div class="mt-14 max-w-prose" data-reveal>
		<h3 class="text-h3 font-semibold">And it tells you what it cannot know</h3>
		<p class="mt-4 text-ink-soft">
			This is the app&rsquo;s own wording, on its own screen, about its own suggestion:
		</p>
		<!-- Quoted verbatim from LearnedYieldReview.svelte. If that string ever
		     changes, this changes with it or it comes off the page. A product
		     quoting itself inaccurately about its own honesty is worse than not
		     making the claim. -->
		<blockquote
			class="mt-5 border-l-2 border-amber-deep pl-5 text-lede text-ink-soft italic"
		>
			This is inferred from buying per planned batch. It can include over-portioning or
			extra batches that were not recorded; it is not a measurement of what went into one
			batch.
		</blockquote>
		<p class="mt-5 text-ink-soft">
			It could have said nothing. Anyone sharp enough to spot that limit was going to
			spot it in the demo anyway, and a number that admits what it is built on is the
			only kind worth putting a price on.
		</p>
	</div>
</section>
